::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 GP Q01::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Scenario: A student will speak to classmates about why everyone should bring a notebook to advisory. What is the speaker's main purpose? {
~A. To entertain classmates with a funny story#Incorrect. The scenario does not focus on humor or storytelling; it asks the speaker to explain why classmates should do something.
=B. To encourage classmates to bring a notebook#Correct. The speaker is trying to influence classmates to take a specific action, so this identifies the purpose.
~C. To summarize a long article about notebooks#Incorrect. No article is being summarized, and the task is about preparing an oral message.
~D. To compare several school supplies#Incorrect. The scenario names one action, bringing a notebook, rather than comparing multiple supplies.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 GP Q02::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.L.01
Scenario: A student is preparing a short presentation for school administrators about adding more quiet study space after school. Which language choice best fits the audience? {
~A. Quiet rooms are awesome and everybody knows it.#Incorrect. This is too casual and unsupported for school administrators.
~B. We need more places because students are tired.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not clearly fit a formal school audience.
~C. People should just let us study somewhere.#Incorrect. This sounds informal and demanding instead of audience-aware.
=D. Additional quiet study space could support students who need focused time before leaving campus.#Correct. This wording is formal, specific, and appropriate for administrators.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 GP Q03::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.10
Scenario: Topic: school clubs. Audience: ninth-grade students. Which sentence states the clearest perspective? {
=A. Joining a school club can help ninth-grade students meet people and feel connected to school.#Correct. This is a focused point of view that can guide a presentation.
~B. This presentation is about school clubs.#Incorrect. This only names the topic and does not give a clear perspective.
~C. There are many different school clubs.#Incorrect. This is a fact-like statement, but it does not show the speaker's point of view.
~D. School clubs happen after school on different days.#Incorrect. This gives a detail, not a focused perspective.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.01 U06 L01 GP Q04::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Scenario: A speaker is addressing parents about student organization habits. Which phrase uses academic vocabulary appropriately? {
~A. Staying on top of stuff#Incorrect. This phrase is conversational and vague, so it does not fit academic speaking well.
~B. Doing things better#Incorrect. This phrase is too general and does not clearly communicate the idea.
=C. Developing consistent organization routines#Correct. This phrase uses precise academic vocabulary appropriate for a formal audience.
~D. Getting it together#Incorrect. This is informal and would weaken a prepared oral presentation.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 GP Q05::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Scenario: A student plans to explain recycling rules to new students. Which planning note best includes purpose, audience, and perspective? {
~A. Recycling rules are posted near the cafeteria.#Incorrect. This is only a detail and does not include the full speaking plan.
=B. I will explain to new students how following recycling rules keeps shared spaces cleaner.#Correct. This note includes purpose, audience, and a clear perspective.
~C. New students need to hear a presentation in the cafeteria.#Incorrect. This names an audience and place but does not state a focused perspective.
~D. My topic is recycling at school.#Incorrect. This only names the topic and leaves the purpose and perspective unclear.
}
